EXT. FROZEN RIVER GORGE - DUSK

A slot of black rock, ice-glazed. Wind scours the canyon floor. Snow hisses along the ridge like sand.

At the bottom, half under an overhang, a collapsed tent: orange nylon sagging under a week of drift. Beside it, a dead stove and a rope tied to nothing.

DR. WILHELMINA OSEI-STRAND (40s, glaciologist, frostbitten cheeks, a parka two sizes too big) crouches beside her younger colleague, TOMAS BRENNAN-ADEYEMI (20s, field assistant, one boot missing), who sits propped against the rock with his eyes half open.

WILHELMINA
(to Tomas)
Talk to me. Count backward from ten.

TOMAS
(slurring)
Ten. Nine.

WILHELMINA
Keep going.

TOMAS
Eight. Tired.

She tucks her scarf around his throat. Stands. Cups her mouth toward the ridge.

WILHELMINA (CONT'D)
(shouting, to the wind)
Anyone! Anyone up there!

Only the wind answers.

She lowers her hands. Mutters, barely audible.

WILHELMINA (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Okay. Okay. Think. Fire. Heat. Water.

She digs through the wreck of the tent and finds a single flare, cracked at the base. She weighs it in her glove.

WILHELMINA (CONT'D)
(to Tomas)
We've got one shot. When I say, close your eyes.

TOMAS
Why?

WILHELMINA
Because I'm going to try to be a lighthouse.

She strikes. The flare sputters, catches. Red light floods the walls of the gorge, and the falling snow turns to rubies.

Far above, a faint, answering glow.
